te and subtract `borrow.value` from `supply.value` before adding the result to backing. For USDC, the net value should be converted through the USDC L1-to-EVM conversion. For non-USDC assets, both positive and negative sides should be valued consistently with the same oracle and decimal path used for supplied collateral.\n\nA simplified direction is:\n\n```solidity\nstruct BorrowLendState {\n    uint64 borrowBasis;\n    uint64 borrowValue;\n    uint64 supplyBasis;\n    uint64 supplyValue;\n}\n\nfunction borrowLendBalance(address account, uint64 token)\n    internal view returns (BorrowLendState memory state)\n{\n    (bool ok, bytes memory res) = HyperCoreConstants.PRECOMPILE_SUPPLIED_BALANCE.staticcall(\n        abi.encode(account, token)\n    );\n    require(ok && res.length >= 128, \"PrecompileReader: borrow-lend read failed\");\n    (\n        state.borrowBasis,\n        state.borrowValue,\n        state.supplyBasis,\n        state.supplyValue\n    ) = abi.decode(res, (uint64, uint64, uint64, uint64));\n}\n```\n\nThen `_readL1Backing()` should add the signed net value:\n\n```solidity\nnet = supplyValue - borrowValue\n```\n\nThe protocol should also ensure that all tokens that can create Borrow-Lend liabilities are either registered and netted, or that settlement reverts when an untracked Borrow-Lend liability exists.\n\n---\n\n## Why this is Medium under Code4rena criteria\n\nThis issue is Medium because it creates a material error in core backing and yield-settlement accounting.\n\nThe proof of concept shows that:\n\n* `totalBackingSigned()` is inflated by ignored debt\n* `surplus()` and `distributableSurplus()` are inflated\n* `settleDailyPnL()` accepts yield based on phantom surplus\n* `settle()` moves real USDC into `YieldEscrow`\n* the correct net surplus becomes negative after settlement\n\nThis is more than an incorrect view and more than a minor accounting mismatch. It causes real USDC to be routed as yield when the protocol has no net surplus.\n\nIt is not High because the path is not a direct permissionless drain, the settlement is still bounded by the yield pipeline, and the issue depends on a Borrow-Lend liability existing in the Vault's Portfolio Margin state.\n\n---\n\n## Why this issue is in scope\n\nThe issue relies only on in-scope contracts:\n\n* [`PrecompileReader.sol`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/3d94be1361ca01d959f9165a78f0d75c5657fe3e/scope.txt#L6)\n* [`MonetrixAccountant.sol`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/3d94be1361ca01d959f9165a78f0d75c5657fe3e/scope.txt#L3)\n* [`MonetrixVault.sol`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/3d94be1361ca01d959f9165a78f0d75c5657fe3e/scope.txt#L5)\n* [`TokenMath.sol`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/3d94be1361ca01d959f9165a78f0d75c5657fe3e/scope.txt#L8)\n* [`ActionEncoder.sol`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/3d94be1361ca01d959f9165a78f0d75c5657fe3e/scope.txt#L1)\n* [`HyperCoreConstants.sol`](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-04-monetrix/blob/3d94be1361ca01d959f9165a78f0d75c5657fe3e/scope.txt#L13)\n\nThe proof of concept uses mocks only to provide well-formed HyperCore precompile responses. It does not depend on out-of-scope contracts for the root cause. The vulnerable behavior is in the in-scope accounting path that counts 0x811 supply values but ignores 0x811 borrow liabilities.",
